   "subject": "Need advice for using mapreduce() to mimic tapply() in producing random sets of matrices.",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<br>Hello,<br>I am trying to setup an experiment where upon I fit a model to a giant block of data split up into various size subsets and find out how timings vary with subsets.  I am new to Hadoop but not R.<br>So to do this I started with a toy problem of trying to create, 4 2*3 matrices of random numbers, where each matrix is a value associated with keys 1, 2, 3 and 4.<br>Using tapply I would do this:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">num </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">24</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>keys </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>tapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">num</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> keys</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nrow </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>So I want to mimic this using map reduce.<br>The first issue I found was that mapreduce() requires an input on the hdfs.  Okay, so I just send a random object to hdfs and then don&#39;t use it.<br>What I came up with the following.<br><br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">gen</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">map </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">v</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">num </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">24</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">dim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  key </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  kv </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> NULL<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> key</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    kv$key </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">keys</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    kv$val </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">values</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">num</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[,,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>a </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mapreduce</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  input </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> input</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  map </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gen</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">map<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div>This works, but this feels wrong.  I suspect it is inefficient.  I feel like I need a reduce step where I break \r\nup the randomly generated numbers into matrices according to their keys,\r\n just like in tapply, but I can&#39;t see the solution -- still all very \r\nnew.  Can someone offer some advice?<br><br><br><br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "jxP5pI5KYosJ",
   "from": "Antonio Piccolboni",
   "author": "6ac7e7dfbe4f6c38",
   "date": "2012-11-11T17:57:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: [RHadoop:#361] Need advice for using mapreduce() to mimic tapply() in producing random sets of matrices.",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Sun, Nov 11, 2012 at 8:35 AM, Robert Ness <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rober...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n<br>Hello,<br>I am trying to setup an experiment where upon I fit a model to a giant block of data split up into various size subsets and find out how timings vary with subsets.  I am new to Hadoop but not R.<br>So to do this I started with a toy problem of trying to create, 4 2*3 matrices of random numbers, where each matrix is a value associated with keys 1, 2, 3 and 4.<br>\r\n\r\nUsing tapply I would do this:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>num </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">24</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br>\r\n\r\nkeys </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br>\r\n\r\ntapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>num</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> keys</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span> nrow </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n<br></span></div></code></div><br>So I want to mimic this using map reduce.<br>The first issue I found was that mapreduce() requires an input on the hdfs.  Okay, so I just send a random object to hdfs and then don&#39;t use it.<br>\r\n\r\n</blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>That will prevent you from achieving any parallelization. Yes you are running on mapreduce, but with a single map process, so what is the point?</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\nWhat I came up with the following.<br><br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span>gen</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>map </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>v</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n  r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>num </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> array</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>rnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">24</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span>dim</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span>c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n  key </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span><br>  kv </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> NULL<br>\r\n\r\n  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> key</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span><br>    kv$key </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>keys</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span> i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n    kv$val </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>values</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>num</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[,,</span><span>i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]))</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>kv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div>\r\n\r\n</code></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Yes the for loop where you add one element at a time to the key vector is about as slow R code as you can possibly write, barring deliberate time waste. This is independent of  rmr. Also I don&#39;t recommend accessing the representation of a keyval object with $key and $val. I can&#39;t prevent you from doing so in R, but the representation can change any time. The supported interface is keys() and values(). Those are not left values, so you can&#39;t assign to them. But there is a simpler way of doing it, just call keyval(key, r.num). Unfortunately rmr doesn&#39;t support arrays yet, but you could make r.num a list of matrices, the list of the same length as the number of keys.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\">\r\n\r\n<code><div><span><br>a </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span> mapreduce</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span><br>  input </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> input</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span><br>\r\n\r\n  map </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span> gen</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span>map<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br></span></div></code></div>This works, but this feels wrong.  I suspect it is inefficient. </blockquote>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>It is, but not because of the missing reducer.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"> I feel like I need a reduce step where I break \r\nup the randomly generated numbers into matrices according to their keys,\r\n just like in tapply,</blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>That&#39;s not for efficiency, it&#39;s if you want the mapreduce program to be functionally equivalent to the sequential one. You are fundmentally right, reduce is equivalent to tapply.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div><br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"> but I can&#39;t see the solution -- still all very \r\nnew.  Can someone offer some advice?</blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>First switch to a local backend, then&#39;t it&#39;s all local and you can debug map and reduce and see what happens. When you are ready for a big run, switch back to hadoop.  Second, you need to make your program closer to the tapply, that is it needs a reduce function  and it needs to use the tapply INDEX as keys. Next you need to create a list of matrices because we don&#39;t have support of arrays yet. In the reduce function you&#39;ll get a list of matrices as the second argument, you probably know better than I do how to go from there to an array. Maybe at some other time we need to discuss the specs to add support for arrays at the same level as there is for matrices or data frames. I hope this helps</div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div>Antonio </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n\r\n<br><br><br>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\npost:  <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rha...@googlegroups.com</a> ||<br>\r\nunsubscribe: <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rhadoop+u...@googlegroups.com</a> ||<br>\r\nweb: <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/forum/rhadoop?hl=en-US\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/forum/rhadoop?hl%3Den-US&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792493086589000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0EIMEnTdhL6XzXeoWbkdzl\">https://groups.google.com/d/forum/rhadoop?hl=en-US</a><br>\r\n <br>\r\n <br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
